<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mudéjar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th–16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Mudejars, Mudéjars, Mudéjares</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mudéjars were Muslims who remained under Christian rule in the Iberian kingdoms as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> advanced, from the 11th to the early 16th century. Protected by terms of surrender, they kept their religion and law in return for tribute and formed much of the rural population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>. Forced conversion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> from 1502 and in the Crown of Aragon in the 1520s turned them into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mudejar.jpg" alt="Mudéjar" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tagarino · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torre_de_El_Salvador._Teruel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
